-- Guided Childhood — Migration 115
-- The England English curriculum, years 1 to 6.
--
-- Companion to 114. Source: National curriculum in England, English programmes
-- of study, the GOV.UK version updated 16 July 2014, supplied by Justin on
-- 27 July. 113 statutory requirements, landing as 220 rows once the key stage 2
-- year pairs are expanded. Key stages 3 and 4 are in the same document and are
-- deliberately excluded: these sheets are years 1 to 6.
--
-- ── Two things here are genuinely different from maths ───────────────────────
--
-- FIRST, THE TERM IS 'all', NOT A TERM.
--
-- Maths gets a term because schools do sequence it. English does not. The
-- programmes of study set reading and writing out for the whole year and no
-- school teaches handwriting in the spring and stops. Putting these rows in a
-- term would be inventing a fact, which is the exact failure this table is
-- shaped to prevent, so they carry the term 'all' and the sheet reads them
-- whatever the date is. The page says so on the sheet rather than leaving a
-- parent to assume the heading means their child's term.
--
-- SECOND, KEY STAGE 2 IS WRITTEN IN PAIRS.
--
-- English is set out year by year for key stage 1 and TWO YEARLY for key stage
-- 2: one programme of study for years 3 and 4, one for years 5 and 6. That is
-- the document, not an omission. So the years 3 and 4 requirements are inserted
-- against year 3 AND year 4, and likewise 5 and 6, and each row's source says
-- 'years 3 and 4' rather than pretending to be year specific. A Year 3 sheet
-- and a Year 4 sheet are therefore the same sheet, which is correct: the
-- statutory expectation is the same for both.
--
-- Spoken language is the same again, one set of 12 requirements that applies
-- across all six years, inserted against every one of them.
--
-- ── Reading is its own subject, English is the rest ──────────────────────────
--
-- The printables page already had three tabs. Reading word reading and reading
-- comprehension go to 'reading' because that is what a parent means by it.
-- Writing, spelling, handwriting and spoken language go to 'english'.
--
-- ── What the GOV.UK rendering broke, and what was done about it ──────────────
--
-- The 2014 web version renders links to the appendices as raw markup, and the
-- debris lands in the middle of the statutory text: bare paths beginning
-- /government/uploads, and trailing fragments like _Spelling.pdf). The same
-- breakage duplicates a word in the year 1 grammar line, which reads 'in
-- English English appendix 2' where every other year reads 'in English appendix
-- 2'. All of it is stripped. It is rendering debris, not wording, and it is the
-- only editing done to any line.
--
-- Structure was checked against the September 2013 PDF, as it was for maths.
--
-- ── Supabase editor rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Idempotent creates, no DO blocks, flat statements only, and no semicolons
-- inside string literals: the dashboard splits statements naively and a
-- semicolon in copy breaks the paste. The statutory text is full of them, so
-- they are spliced with chr(59) and the stored value stays byte for byte the
-- published text.

on conflict (curriculum, year_group, subject, term, objective) do nothing;

